;; Reviewed 2026-10-10 by the user (written by Claude, from the design,
;; the MACRO manual, LLVM's code generator and the debugger runs). Why
;; this is right:
;; - The main module of a program of two: poc -emit-macro32
;;   VaxProcVals.mod writes it and its import's VaxProcLib.mar
;;   (expected-vax-VaxProcLib.mar), assembled and linked together.
;; - Phase 16 step 3 (section 14, item 7): a procedure value is the
;;   longword address of a procedure's entry mask, and NIL is 0, so a
;;   variable, field or element of procedure type is a longword: .BLKL,
;;   MOVL, CLRL for NIL. A procedure taken as a value is MOVAB of its
;;   symbol, a private procedure's local label at its .WORD mask (Add,
;;   Bump) or an exported one's .ENTRY, G^ for another module's (L.Max).
;;   MOVAB writes straight into the variable, or the argument list's
;;   entry when the procedure is an argument (Run(Bump, 3)).
;; - = and # compare two values, or one and NIL, as longwords: CMPL, or
;;   TSTL against NIL.
;; - A call through a value evaluates the value first, as LLVM does,
;;   into R2-R5, which the call does not have to move, and checks it for
;;   NIL: BNEQ over POC_TRAP with code 4 at the designator's line and
;;   column. The argument list follows from the procedure type's
;;   parameters, as for a direct call: a VAR record parameter's tag
;;   (ins(circle)), an open array's length (summer, measurer) and a
;;   HUGEINT's copy (wide). Then CALLG list, (Rn), or CALLS #0, (Rn)
;;   with no arguments (act).
;; - Calls through values in another's arguments (nested, line 225) keep
;;   each value in its own register, R2, R3 and R4, across the inner
;;   calls, and each argument list in its own slots of the frame.
;; - A function's procedure result comes back in R0 (Pick, chooser,
;;   L.Hidden), and a VAR parameter of procedure type is its address
;;   (Install's slot, given f and table[3]).
;; - The debugger runs examine what it computes, which ProcValsOut,
;;   built by both backends, also prints, and what four variables hold,
;;   which the debugger names by their procedures. Then trap 4 from a
;;   NIL variable at column 35, and from a NIL element of a pointer's
;;   array at column 25, the designator, as LLVM's -trap-location gives
;;   them (since 63f53c1 it no longer gives an element's index, 39).
;; - NEW calls rtl/vax's GarbageCollectedHeap.Allocate by its symbol,
;;   G^GARBAGECOLLECTEDHEAP_A_A8NV38EI, .EXTERNAL, where it called
;;   POC_NEW (Phase 16 step 4, section 15, proposal 9): the same
;;   argument list, the size and the tag, and the same result in R0.
;; - Its variables hold pointers, so it has a root table, L_ROOTS in
;;   POC_DATA: a link, which POC_ROOTS sets, the count, 2, and the
;;   address of each pointer (machine and head), flattened as a type
;;   descriptor's offsets are. The initializer registers it with
;;   POC_ROOTS before it calls its imports' initializers.
;; - The program allocates, so its start calls the collector's
;;   initializer, GARBAGECOLLECTEDHEAP_INIT, and then SetStackBase
;;   (GARBAGECOLLECTEDHEAP_S_EA118OLO) with its own FP, the base the
;;   collector scans the stack up to, before the module's initializer.
;; - The debugger runs link tools/vax-runtime-stub.mar, whose Allocate
;;   takes memory from LIB$GET_VM as POC_NEW did and never collects.
; VaxProcVals, written by poc (doc/developer/vax-macro32-backend.md)
	.TITLE	VAXPROCVALS
